www.nature.com/scientificreports/
on the northern side of the Himalayas and runs southwest across the Assam Valley, is the second biggest river
laden with silt following the Huang He in China15,16. The quality of groundwater over the whole floodplain is
greatly impacted by the influx from the contributing rivers and nearby catchment regions.
Due to significant carcinogenic threat posed by As, the World Health Organization (WHO) lowered the
permissible limit for As in drinking water to 10 µg L−1 in 1993 (replacing the previous threshold of 50 µg L−1)17.
Several Indian states, particularly those located in the Ganga–Brahmaputra flood plains, are witnessing increased
levels of As concentrations in groundwater (> 10 µg L−1), as highlighted by several researchers4–10,18–21. The As
concentrations surpassed limits (WHO) in Assam’s Lakhimpur, Nagaon, Jorhat, Golaghat and Majuli5–10.
Several studies examining the amount of As in drinking water have revealed that, in addition to skin lesions,
it may raise the risk of lung cancer, hypertension, and diabetes mellitus (nine folds higher) in humans22,23.
Epidemiologic studies illustrating a correlation between As concentration and cancer, frequently rely on measures
relating to past exposure through various pathways24. However, drinking As contaminated water stands to be the
greatest threats to human health, as documented by national and international cases25. It is currently believed
that the primary mechanism for releasing As is the reductive dissolution of iron (Fe) and aluminium (Al) metal
oxides along with the activity of native metal-reducing bacteria, which directly affects As mobility26.
On the other hand, dental caries, fluorosis (including dental, skeletal, and crippling), and renal damage are
all brought on by fluoride (F) in groundwater at concentrations higher than those advised27. Fluoride intake over
recommended levels has also been linked to infertility, hypertension, abortion, and fertility27. The Brahmaputra
valley is known for its high groundwater F concentrations, which are mostly derived from the parent rock and
are mobilized by leaching and desorption in an alkaline environment15,21,27,28.
In the Darrang district of Assam, groundwater plays an indispensable role in meeting the water needs of
its burgeoning population and driving agricultural productivity29. However, the quality of groundwater in this
region is increasingly under scrutiny due to a confluence of natural and anthropogenic factors, posing significant
challenges to its sustainable management and utilization. Located in the flood-prone Brahmaputra Valley,
Darrang district faces unique hydrogeological dynamics influenced by the region’s topography, geology, and
climatic conditions. The association between surface water interactions, sediment transport, and groundwater
recharge processes shapes the distribution and quality of groundwater resources in the area29. Additionally,
rapid developmental activities, agricultural practices, and inadequate sanitation infrastructure contribute to
the contamination of groundwater with pollutants. The reliance on groundwater for domestic and agricultural
purposes highlights the urgency of assessing and safeguarding its quality in Darrang.
In the present study, the occurrence and spatial distribution of geogenic contaminants in shallow groundwater
aquifers of Darrang district has been evaluated. Moreover, the potential carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic
health risks associated with As enriched groundwater were also estimated. To the best of our knowledge, no
published literature from Darrang has conducted extensive sampling to evaluate health risks related to geogenic
contaminants along with the spatial distribution and dynamics of these contaminants in Darrang, Assam.
Our work aims to bridge the knowledge gap on the distribution, sources, and health risks in the Brahmaputra
floodplains of Assam.

Materials and methods
Study area

The district has a total of 1585 square km placed in between 20°9′ N to 26°027′ N latitude and 91°045′ E to
92°022′ E longitude. Arunachal Pradesh, Bhutan, and Udalguri district form the northern borders of Darrang
area. Sonitpur and Kamrup districts are located in the east and west, respectively. The district has a population
of 928,500 people as per the 2011 census data. The district’s net sown area covers 73,619 hectares, making up
46.25% of its total geographic area. Paddy is the dominant crop, with three primary types cultivated: autumn,
winter, and summer varieties. Among these, winter paddy is the most extensively grown, accounting for 70.6%
of the district’s paddy cultivation29.
The district experiences a subtropical, humid climate with a pronounced seasonal variation. Humidity
remains consistently high throughout the year, reaching approximately 90% during the rainy season. Winter
begins in November and lasts until February, with December and January being the coldest months, where
temperatures can drop to approximately 15 °C. From February or March, temperatures start to rise, reaching
their peak in July or August, with highs of around 40 °C. Annual rainfall averaging 1951 mm, of which 60–65%
falls between June and September during the southwest monsoon. Additionally, the district receives around
501 mm of rainfall during the pre-monsoon period from March to May, often as thunderstorms or hailstorms29.

Physiography, geology and geomorphology

Physiographically, the district consists of an alluvial plain with flat topography that gently slopes toward the
Brahmaputra River, forming its southern boundary. The district’s soil cover includes both younger and older
alluvium, which has undergone significant pedological variation. These soils are characterized by medium to
high levels of organic carbon, along with low to medium phosphate and potassium content, influencing the
area’s agricultural and ecological conditions. From a geological perspective, the entire district is made up of
Quaternary sediments, both younger and older. The older alluvium, which is primarily composed of sand,
gravel, pebbles, and clay, covers the northern foothill region, while the newer alluvium in the south is primarily
composed of silt, clay, and fine sand29,30.
The geomorphology of the area is divided into three main zones: flood plain, younger alluvial plain, and
inselberg. The flood plains, primarily along the Brahmaputra River and its tributaries, consist of loose gravel,
sand, silt, and clay. These regions are shaped by seasonal flooding, creating features like paleo channels, natural
levees, back swamps, wetlands, and channel bars, which are conducive to shallow groundwater development. The
younger alluvial plain, occupying most of the district, sits at a slightly higher elevation than the flood plain and
