shares similar sediment composition. The inselberg zone, confined to a small area in the extreme southwestern
part of the district, contains granite and granite gneisses as isolated hills, where groundwater can be accessed
through dug wells in weathered areas30.

Aquifer characteristics

Groundwater is found in shallow aquifers under unconfined conditions, while deeper aquifers are semi-confined
to confined. During the pre-monsoon period, groundwater levels typically range between 2 to 4 m below ground
level (bgl) across most of the district. In the post-monsoon period, water levels rise to between 1 and 2 m bgl,
with some areas experiencing water levels above ground29. Groundwater flows from the higher elevations in the
northwest toward the Brahmaputra River in the southeast. The water table reaches a maximum of 80 m above
mean sea level30.
Over the past decade, long-term monitoring reveals a pattern of rising water levels during the post-monsoon
season, followed by a decline in the pre-monsoon period. However, no significant fluctuation was indicated in
the groundwater levels within the district during the decade. Darrang has a substantial groundwater resource
in a number of regionally widespread aquifers owing to physiographical, geological, and climatic advantageous
conditions31.

Sampling process and analytical design

A total of 347 groundwater samples were collected from the entire Darrang district, Assam in 2023. The
sampling zones were chosen to represent a range of geological features. Groundwater samples from shallow
tubewells were collected using stratified random sampling method (grab sampling) covering all the community
development blocks (7 nos.) of Darrang district viz. Bechimari, Dalgaon-Sialmari, Khairabari, Sipajhar, Pashim
Mangaldoi, Pub Mangaldoi and Kalaigaon. Over 86 grids measuring an area of 16 km2/grid (4 km × 4 km) and

